To generate an automatically generated table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document, you can use the `tocloft` package and the following commands:

1. Add the `tocloft` package to your document preamble:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft}
% rest of your preamble
```

2. Use the `tocloft` commands to customize the appearance of the TOC:

```latex
% Set the spacing between TOC entries
\setlength{\cftbeforetoctitleskip}{0pt}
\setlength{\cftaftertoctitleskip}{12pt}
\setlength{\cftbeforesecskip}{6pt}
\setlength{\cftbeforesubsecskip}{6pt}

% Set the font for TOC entries
\renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\normalsize\bfseries}
\renewcommand{\cftsubsecfont}{\normalsize}
```

3. Add the `\tableofcontents` command to your document, usually at the beginning of the document, after the `\begin{document}` command:

```latex
\begin{document}
\tableofcontents
% rest of your document
```

4. Add the section and subsection commands to your document, as needed:

```latex
\section{First Section}
\subsection{First Subsection}
% rest of your document
```

With these commands, your TOC will be automatically generated based on the section and subsection headings in your document. 

Here's a complete example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft}

% Customize TOC appearance
\setlength{\cftbeforetoctitleskip}{0pt}
\setlength{\cftaftertoctitleskip}{12pt}
\setlength{\cftbeforesecskip}{6pt}
\setlength{\cftbeforesubsecskip}{6pt}

\renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\normalsize\bfseries}
\renewcommand{\cftsubsecfont}{\normalsize}

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\section{First Section}
\subsection{First Subsection}

\section{Second Section}
\subsection{Second Subsection}

\end{document}
``` 

This will generate a TOC that looks like:

```
Contents
--------
1. First Section
   1.1 First Subsection
2. Second Section
   2.1 Second Subsection
```

To draw a chessboard in LaTeX, you can use the `chessboard` package. Here's a minimal example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chessboard}

\begin{document}

\chessboard[fen="rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq"]

\end{document}
```

This will output a chessboard with the starting position (white's side up). To use a different FEN notation, simply replace the `fen` argument with your desired FEN string.

Note: The `chessboard` package requires the `tikz` package, so make sure to add `\usepackage{tikz}` to the preamble if you haven't already.

